  "worked_solution": "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(ay(1+x){e}^{x}+y{e}^{y}+4) \\\\  & =a(1+x){e}^{x}+(1+y){e}^{y} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(x(1+y){e}^{y}-bx{e}^{x}-1) \\\\  & =(1+y){e}^{y}-b(1+x){e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1+x){e}^{x}+(1+y){e}^{y}=(1+y){e}^{y}-b(1+x){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $(1+x){e}^{x},$ respectively, we see that $a=-b.$ \n\nNow, since ${f}_{x}(1,1)=4+5e$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =4+5e \\\\ a(1)(1+(1))e+(1)e+4 & =4+5e \\\\ 2ae+e+4 & =4+5e \\\\ (2a+1)e & =5e \\\\ 2a+1 & =5 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nb=-a=-2.\n$$",
